# GNOME Shell Todo Extension — project context (local goosehints)

A GNOME Shell 46 todo extension written in JavaScript (ESModules).
Target: GNOME 46 only. Session: X11. Code language: English.

## Data / architecture
- Tasks are stored in a plain-text **markdown checkbox** file: `~/todo.md`
  - Format: `- [ ] task` (incomplete) and `- [x] task` (complete)
  - Standalone file (not Obsidian) -> anyone can use it (markdown is universal).
- Keep UI (panel/menus), storage (file I/O), and business logic in separate
  functions/files. Do not pile everything into one giant function.

## Coding rules (to prevent spaghetti, from the start)
- Always use official and trusted resources, don't make guess. Here is the some resources for GNOME extensions:
  - https://gjs.guide/
  - https://gjs-docs.gnome.org/
  - https://gjs.guide/extensions/development/
  
- KISS: no unnecessary abstraction, design patterns, or "we'll need it later" code.
- DRY — rule of three: copying twice is fine; extract a shared helper on the 3rd repeat.
- Keep files small and single-responsibility; do not dogmatically force SOLID.
- "Don't break what works": new code must not break existing structure.
- Do not use legacy/Clutter/`imports` API — GNOME 45+ ESM and Shell's `St`/`misc` only.
  Do not raise `shell-version` in `metadata.json` above `["46"]`; don't claim to
  support versions you haven't tested.

### VERIFY-BEFORE-WRITE (hard rule — learned from the edit failure)
This project repeatedly broke because APIs were guessed instead of verified.
The edit feature had to be reverted after a pile of invented methods. To prevent it:
- **No GJS/GNOME Shell API call is written on assumption.** Every St / Clutter /
  PopupMenu / Gio / Main API must be checked against gjs.guide or GNOME GitLab
  BEFORE it goes into code. An unverified API never enters a commit.
  This echo of the old "don't invent" rule is now a hard gate, not a hint.
- When the intended API name differs from GNOME/GTK habits (set_hexpand vs
  x_expand, key::release vs key-release-event, CSS opacity vs actor opacity),
  STOP and surface the verified name rather than guessing.
- **Known-verified patterns (do NOT re-derive or "improve"):**
  - `set_x_expand(true)` + `set_x_align(Clutter.ActorAlign.START/END)` for
    expand/align on St actors (NOT GTK `hexpand`/`set_hexpand`).
  - St.Entry Enter handling: `connect('key-release-event', (entry, event) => {
    if (event.get_key_symbol() === Clutter.KEY_Return) ... })`
    (NOT a `key::release` GObject signal).
  - `Gio.File.replace_contents(bytes, ...)` takes a `Uint8Array` (yielded by
    `new TextEncoder().encode(content)`), NOT `GLib.Bytes`.
  - Fade completed labels via actor `label.opacity`, not CSS `opacity` (unreliable
    on St.Label); line-through via `stylesheet.css` `.todo-done`.
  - File reading uses `new TextDecoder().decode(bytes)`.
- **Stop-and-flag rule:** if an API fix does not work within a couple of attempts,
  do NOT stack more guessed fixes (this is how the edit fiasco snowballed). Stop,
  look up the upstream source/docs, or revert to the last known-good state.

### Where state-changing logic lives
- All file mutation (add / toggle / delete / edit / archive) is a **pure function
  in `storage.js`** returning the updated content string. `extension.js` only reads
  the file, calls the storage function, writes back, then rebuilds the UI.
- `extension.js` stays a thin UI layer. Do NOT move parsing/writing logic into it
  — the Phase-1 edit failure exactly violates this boundary.

## Phase plan (priority order — desktop-pinned widget is a LATER phase)
- Phase 1: core — read/list `~/todo.md` + add/delete + check/uncheck, clickability
- Phase 2: native notifications, archiving
- Phase 3: keyboard shortcut
- Phase 4: desktop-pinned widget (NOT the first priority)

## Testing / discipline
- Test after each feature/compile: reload Shell (Alt+F2 -> r) + manual verification.
- Error tracking: Looking Glass (Alt+F2 -> `lg`). `journalctl -f` is also usable;
  `journalctl -u gnome-shell*` does NOT work on this system — don't use it.
- Optional safe testing: `dbus-run-session -- gnome-shell --nested --wayland`.
- Small, single-feature steps; don't add multiple features in one message.
- Commit early and often; each commit message should summarize the change.

### Tests
- Run `tests/run_tests.mjs` (pure `storage.js` logic) after every storage change.
- Run `tests/smoke.sh` (ext is still `State: ACTIVE`) after every UI change.
- Principle: logic that can be pure goes in `storage.js` and is unit-tested (no GJS
  runtime needed). Logic that is inherently UI stays in `extension.js` and is only
  smoke-checked — its correctness comes from VERIFY-BEFORE-WRITE, not from tests.

### Commit hygiene
- ONE concern per commit. Do not bundle `fix + style + polish` together; it makes
  history unreadable and reverts hard.
- Commit message summarizes exactly the one change (see git log for the convention).

## Process / interaction
- Before starting a new feature, produce a short checklist/plan (list the steps) and
  wait for my approval; don't start coding before I approve.
- Before finishing a step, use `/goal` with a clear, testable completion criterion.
- After a change, tell me EXACTLY how to test it (command + steps); don't proceed to
  the next step until I test and approve.
- Summarize the diff; we are in approve mode, don't proceed without approval.
- Approval before action: present changes to me before applying them.
